        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold mb-2 text-lightblue">Night-Diving in Bali — A Field Guide</h2>
        <p class="text-white/80">From Tulamben's sleeping giants to Padang Bai's macro-critters, this guide covers Bali's top night-diving sites, safety protocols, gear essentials, and what you can expect to see when the sun goes down.</p>
      </header>

      <article class="prose prose-invert max-w-none">
        <p>Slipping beneath the surface as darkness falls is like entering an entirely new dimension. The familiar reefs you explored by day transform. The daytime crowds of fish retreat into crevices, and a new cast of nocturnal predators and bizarre critters emerges from the shadows. Night-diving in Bali isn't just diving in the dark; it's witnessing the ocean's second act.</p>
        <p>This guide is built for the practical diver. We'll skip the fluff and focus on actionable intelligence: where to go, what to look for, and how to do it safely.</p>

        <h3 class="text-gold"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-check mr-2"></i>Key Takeaways for Your Bali Night Dive</h3>
        <ul class="marker:text-gold space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Witness the Shift Change:</strong> Discover which marine animals, from Spanish Dancers to hunting moray eels, become active exclusively after sunset.</li>
            <li><strong>Site-Specific Strategies:</strong> Learn why the USAT Liberty Wreck and Padang Bai's Jetty are premier night-diving locations and what makes them unique.</li>
            <li><strong>Safety is Paramount:</strong> Master the non-negotiable protocols, including dive light communication, buddy procedures, and conservative dive planning.</li>
            <li><strong>Gear Up for Darkness:</strong> Understand the essential equipment beyond your standard kit, focusing on primary torches, backups, and marking lights.</li>
            <li><strong>Embrace the Bioluminescence:</strong> Experience one of nature's most magical phenomena—the "living light" that illuminates the water with every movement.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-lightblue"><i class="fa-solid fa-map-location-dot mr-2"></i>Premier Night-Diving Hotspots</h3>
        <p>Bali offers a diverse menu of night-diving experiences. While many sites can be explored after dark, a few stand out for their accessibility, safety, and extraordinary marine life.</p>
        
        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-white mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-ship mr-2"></i>USAT Liberty Wreck, Tulamben</h4>
          <p class="text-white/80">An absolute classic. The easy, predictable shore entry makes it ideal for divers of all levels. By night, the massive wreck becomes a dormitory for a school of giant bumphead parrotfish, often found sleeping in the cargo hold. Your torch beam will pick out hunting moray eels, flashlight fish, and a host of crustaceans. It's a world-class dive site that offers a completely different personality after dark.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-white mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-anchor mr-2"></i>The Jetty, Padang Bai</h4>
          <p class="text-white/80">A macro-photographer's paradise. The pillars of this old jetty are encrusted with life. At night, this artificial reef is a hotspot for the weird and wonderful. Look for ornate ghost pipefish, frogfish camouflaged against sponges, decorator crabs, and the flamboyant Spanish Dancer nudibranch. The calm conditions make it a perfect location for a slow, observant dive focused on finding hidden treasures.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/5">
          <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-white mb-2"><i class="fa-solid fa-fish-fins mr-2"></i>Amed Walls & Pyramids</h4>
          <p class="text-white/80">Known for its healthy coral gardens and artificial reef structures, Amed is another fantastic option. The gentle slopes and pyramid structures provide shelter for a vast array of life. Night dives here often reveal sleeping turtles, various species of shrimp and crabs, and octopuses on the move. The potential for encountering bioluminescent plankton here is particularly high on darker nights.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-lightblue"><i class="fa-solid fa-shield-halved mr-2"></i>Safety Protocols: Your Non-Negotiable Checklist</h3>
        <p>Confidence in the dark comes from solid preparation and discipline. Never treat a night dive like a casual day dive. Adhere strictly to these protocols:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-3">
            <li><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb w-5 text-gold"></i><strong>Redundant Light Sources:</strong> Your primary dive torch is your lifeline. A powerful, reliable model is a must. Equally important is a smaller, securely stowed backup torch. No backup, no dive.</li>
            <li><i class="fa-solid fa-people-group w-5 text-gold"></i><strong>Buddy System & Communication:</strong> Stay close to your buddy. Agree on light signals before you enter the water (e.g., "OK" is a circle, "Attention" is a side-to-side sweep). Attach a chemical light or small marker strobe to your tank valve so your buddy can always track you.</li>
            <li><i class="fa-solid fa-compass w-5 text-gold"></i><strong>Know Your Site:</strong> The best practice is to dive a site during the day before exploring it at night. This gives you a mental map of the layout, entry/exit points, and potential hazards.</li>
            <li><i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-up-from-water w-5 text-gold"></i><strong>Conservative Profiles:</strong> Dive shallower and shorter than you would during the day. Monitor your air and no-decompression limits more frequently. Ascend slowly and conduct a thorough safety stop.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-lightblue" id="faq"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-question mr-2"></i>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
        <div class="space-y-3">
          
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">Is Bali night-diving suitable for beginners?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">Absolutely, provided you're with a professional guide. Reputable dive centers in Bali select calm, shallow sites for initial night-dive experiences, often as part of the Advanced Open Water course. Your guide will manage navigation and safety, allowing you to focus on the unique environment.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">What’s the ideal season for night-diving in Bali?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">The dry season, from April to November, generally offers the best conditions with calmer seas and better visibility. However, Bali's microclimates mean conditions can vary. Always trust the local dive center’s assessment for the specific site on any given day.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">How much does a guided night-dive cost in Bali?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">Prices typically range from $50 to $85 USD per dive. This usually includes a dive guide, tank, weights, and torch rental. Costs can vary based on location (shore vs. boat dive) and package deals. Always ask for a clear breakdown of what's included.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">What essential gear do I need for night-diving?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">Beyond your standard dive kit, a reliable primary dive torch is mandatory. A smaller backup torch and a tank marker light (a strobe or chemical light) are also essential for safety and visibility to your buddy and the boat.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-4 bg-white/5">
        <button class="w-full flex items-center justify-between text-left" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden')">
          <span class="font-semibold">Can I see bioluminescence while night-diving in Bali?</span>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-sm"></i>
        </button>
        <div class="hidden mt-3 text-white/80">Yes, it's one of the highlights! On many night dives, especially in areas with minimal light pollution, you can signal your guide to turn off all torches. Agitating the water with your hands will reveal a magical display of sparkling bioluminescent plankton.</div>
      </div>        </div>
